{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"02e01007550864ff23b9b6a9b86b17a9cb139b5a6d01c04dcd83013299f35225","slug":"ch03.intro","location":"/ch03/intro.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Chapter 3: Logic Core Reconstruction","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Jin Lei","given":"Jin","family":"Lei"},"name":"Jin Lei","url":"https://jinlei.fewbody.com","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"github":"https://github.com/jinleiphys/atomic","numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"source_url":"https://github.com/jinleiphys/atomic/blob/main/book/en/ch03/intro.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/jinleiphys/atomic/edit/main/book/en/ch03/intro.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"intro.md","url":"/atomic/en/build/intro-9b415a9de2ae63f9b3188d9e71086197.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"When Classical Concepts No Longer Apply","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"sB4yyu6CM7"}],"identifier":"when-classical-concepts-no-longer-apply","label":"When Classical Concepts No Longer Apply","html_id":"when-classical-concepts-no-longer-apply","implicit":true,"key":"EELEuGMmkK"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In everyday experience, an object is either a bullet or a water wave — it cannot be both at once. A bullet has a definite position and trajectory; you can track its every instant with high-speed photography. A water wave spreads across the entire surface of a lake, possessing no single definite “position,” yet it can bend around obstacles, pass through narrow slits, and superpose with another wave to produce interference fringes. Bullets and water waves are two fundamentally different types of physical entities; they obey different equations of motion and follow different behavioral logics. The entire conceptual framework of classical physics is built upon this clean dichotomy: particles are particles, waves are waves, and the two are sharply separated.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"cqmth5o3ix"}],"key":"t64C8WqgBs"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Bohr model achieved remarkable success with the hydrogen atom spectrum, but it remained at its core a classical model patched with quantization: the electron still moved along definite orbits, still possessed well-defined position and velocity — only the allowed orbits had been discretized. The electron was still a “little ball” — a little ball restricted to specific tracks, but a little ball nonetheless. This “semi-classical” picture quickly ran into difficulties when confronting more complex atomic phenomena, and it could not self-consistently answer a fundamental question: why does the electron not radiate when in an “allowed orbit”? Bohr’s quantization condition was an external rule imposed on top of classical mechanics, like hard-coding a few special cases into an otherwise well-functioning piece of software — it works, but nobody knows why.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"NXzYaKiDla"}],"key":"wqw0z77SxD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"What this chapter undertakes is far more radical than patching. We will demolish the very classical concept of “orbit” itself, replacing it with an entirely new mode of description. A microscopic particle is neither a classical “ball” nor a classical “wave”; it inherits properties of both, yet in its behavior transcends both. Its state is no longer described by definite position and velocity, but by a probability density function — the wave function. And there is an unbreakable fundamental limit on how much information you can extract from that function: the Heisenberg uncertainty relation. This is not a minor repair to classical physics, but a complete demolition and reconstruction of the entire conceptual framework.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"KNMkssgEtO"}],"key":"kIpHSia1GI"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Silicon Perspective: From Procedural to Declarative","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"qNosKyGzII"}],"identifier":"silicon-perspective-from-procedural-to-declarative","label":"Silicon Perspective: From Procedural to Declarative","html_id":"silicon-perspective-from-procedural-to-declarative","implicit":true,"key":"xw8hWUwlNA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we liken physical theories to programming paradigms, classical mechanics is a typical form of ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"jiiUA2Rnno"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"procedural programming","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"YFfdjlFaCN"}],"key":"zzwV5rf7NF"},{"type":"text","value":". You supply initial conditions (position and velocity), then use Newton’s equations to compute the particle’s position step by step at each moment in time — much like iterating through a for loop. The core logic of the program is the “trajectory”: the particle is here at ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"zscHqmhC8d"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"t = 0","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span></span></span></span>","key":"ZB9xiXTCsK"},{"type":"text","value":", there at ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"d0s8XqA0oy"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"t = \\Delta t","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = \\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">Δ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span></span></span></span>","key":"aeCol9UMFP"},{"type":"text","value":", and farther away at ","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"s3k0ehdvQy"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"t = 2\\Delta t","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t = 2\\Delta t</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2Δ</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span></span></span></span>","key":"GnA3N4DnBy"},{"type":"text","value":". The trajectory is continuous, deterministic, and traceable. Given the same initial conditions, you always obtain the same trajectory. This is the programming expression of Laplacian determinism: the universe is a pure function with no side effects and no randomness.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"nPhOTgOukf"}],"key":"DTgCzmKqrr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quantum mechanics, by contrast, more closely resembles ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"dGfiZ85Qfs"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"declarative programming","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"sOB7CwcCAp"}],"key":"kUpa8UnS1Y"},{"type":"text","value":". You no longer describe “how” a particle moves, but rather “what” state it is in. The wave function ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"MOW3gq15Fh"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\psi(x)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">ψ</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>","key":"u918cODmTG"},{"type":"text","value":" does not tell you where the particle is — it tells you the probability of finding the particle at each position. You are not computing a trajectory but querying a probability distribution table. The output of the program is no longer a deterministic numerical value but a probability distribution. Running the same “program” (the same initial wave function), each “execution” (measurement) may yield a different result. The core data structure of procedural programming is a coordinate point ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"VKaZ2IkJ0M"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"(x, p)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x, p)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>","key":"OegGbILVXs"},{"type":"text","value":"; the core data structure of declarative programming is an entire function ","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"NdkGhZAhcU"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"\\psi(x)","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">ψ</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span>","key":"SEAR0oSAru"},{"type":"text","value":", which contains far richer information than a single coordinate point, but the ways in which that information can be extracted are subject to far greater constraints.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"CAcVYV6Lz9"}],"key":"GaYetRBjuo"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The cost of this paradigm shift is enormous. The intuition behind procedural programming is the crystallization of thousands of years of human lived experience — we are innately able to track moving objects, predict the trajectories of projectiles, and understand collisions and rebounds. Declarative programming demands that we abandon these intuitions and accept an entirely new “mental operating system”: particles have no trajectories, only probability clouds; measurement is not the passive reading of a pre-existing value but an active alteration of the system’s state; two seemingly contradictory attributes (wave and particle) can coexist within a single entity. Understanding this paradigm shift is the obligatory gateway into the world of quantum mechanics, and it is the central task of this chapter.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"d3niLePpoA"}],"key":"X4wA3ix4F8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We begin with wave-particle duality, tracing how light and electrons revealed their dual nature as both particles and waves in experiments, and how de Broglie’s bold hypothesis extended wave properties to all matter particles. Wave-particle duality is not a vague philosophical concept but a physical fact supported by precise experimental evidence: the same beam of electrons behaves as particles in Compton scattering experiments and as waves in double-slit experiments. We will then delve into the physical origins and mathematical structure of the uncertainty relation, understanding why position and momentum cannot be simultaneously determined with arbitrary precision — this is not a limitation of measurement technology but a design specification of nature’s underlying architecture. Mathematically, the uncertainty relation is entirely equivalent to the time-frequency uncertainty in signal processing, and this cross-disciplinary resonance reveals a profound truth: uncertainty is not a quirk of quantum mechanics but an inevitable feature of any system described in terms of waves. Finally, we will discuss the statistical interpretation of the wave function, understanding its role as a “state database,” and how the intrinsic probabilistic nature of quantum mechanics ignited the most profound intellectual debate in the history of physics between Einstein and Bohr — a debate over whether “God plays dice,” whose final verdict was not settled until the 21st century through experimental tests of Bell’s inequality.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"dHBJzexyMa"}],"key":"bWLfMfFmmO"},{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source Code: Reverse Engineering the Atom","key":"GmgSrRtpFz"}],"urlSource":"/","key":"fi5h80Ebey"}],"key":"hWicH6omQL"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preface","key":"maRmwiFONa"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/preface","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preface","key":"FZsvLRqADc"}],"urlSource":"/preface","dataUrl":"/preface.json","protocol":"file","key":"X2RJ50zT9s"}],"key":"C7AkZR68kE"}],"key":"a1qFfhrm0u"}],"key":"SoNKvkLv3K"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 1: Nuclear Structure of the Atom","key":"LcoBaiZlEI"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch01.intro","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 1 The Nuclear Structure of the Atom","key":"bCgUOVVRua"}],"urlSource":"/ch01.intro","key":"j9Ma7XoHMd"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch01.electron-discovery","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1.1 Discovery of the Electron and Its Fundamental Properties","key":"MlHeyzwKlf"}],"urlSource":"/ch01.electron-discovery","key":"YSUfEzqsIQ"}],"key":"p3Nfpa5uTP"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch01.rutherford-scattering","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1.2 The Nuclear Structure of the Atom","key":"v2rkHLm4nd"}],"urlSource":"/ch01.rutherford-scattering","key":"akpZrCplbe"}],"key":"tq55fsoHtq"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch01.classical-collapse","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1.3 Difficulties of the Planetary Model","key":"kSQkJAzvoO"}],"urlSource":"/ch01.classical-collapse","key":"qeJkRSGTIS"}],"key":"RoypKCP6Yg"}],"key":"rq6t5pLizk"}],"key":"dgyXeOve3m"}],"key":"Chh05iTHeq"}],"key":"bq6Ekq4573"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 2: Digitization of Information","key":"J8d057H9R7"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch02.intro","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 2: Digitization of Information","key":"eGwWUduyu0"}],"urlSource":"/ch02.intro","key":"iyR3IlurZC"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch02.kirchhoff-blackbody","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.1 Kirchhoff’s Law of Thermal Radiation and Blackbody Radiation","key":"NblF98ia8l"}],"urlSource":"/ch02.kirchhoff-blackbody","key":"t8qo1FXift"}],"key":"oYIzz1Drcj"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch02.photoelectric-effect","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.2 The Photoelectric Effect","key":"aRUTQOQ5gM"}],"urlSource":"/ch02.photoelectric-effect","key":"aeGcjemeOH"}],"key":"Fg69wHyhY9"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch02.spectral-data","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.3 Spectral Data and Empirical Formulas","key":"Q0XlYXgek2"}],"urlSource":"/ch02.spectral-data","key":"i1rt3sJypA"}],"key":"UhbCz9VnH4"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch02.bohr-model","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.4 Bohr’s Quantization Postulates","key":"CyKUCEkykw"}],"urlSource":"/ch02.bohr-model","key":"LDmpKWAEPK"}],"key":"IZq7dvLrJH"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch02.franck-hertz","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2.5 The Franck-Hertz Experiment","key":"Cs39ZMMikn"}],"urlSource":"/ch02.franck-hertz","key":"hrCgfyFwKQ"}],"key":"eQFgtW1Tb3"}],"key":"XsF22lYK7P"}],"key":"vWmu69ea6R"}],"key":"zK4bkUCPMB"}],"key":"vXw4gUSKON"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 3: Logic Core Reconstruction","key":"T9HXrgadUR"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch03.intro","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 3: Logic Core Reconstruction","key":"OTzKIWDFCq"}],"urlSource":"/ch03.intro","key":"Pq5t3nsVLY"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch03.wave-particle","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.1 Wave-Particle Duality","key":"mesol0uNwQ"}],"urlSource":"/ch03.wave-particle","key":"oo7NEh6lew"}],"key":"AgCRifYzve"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch03.uncertainty","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.2 The Uncertainty Relation","key":"lM661EmQH0"}],"urlSource":"/ch03.uncertainty","key":"Vi4wjc8fEe"}],"key":"esrdaxiR9P"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch03.wavefunction","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3.3 The Wave Function and the Probability Interpretation","key":"zcydVAKNvX"}],"urlSource":"/ch03.wavefunction","key":"SxfOnDvjAm"}],"key":"gpNndjwRjP"}],"key":"lMPff5RkpB"}],"key":"yPR9UX61NV"}],"key":"uGaHPv8hC8"}],"key":"WoTs2Do02A"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 4: The Ultimate Algorithm","key":"vR1JrKv2zq"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch04.intro","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 4: The Ultimate Algorithm","key":"ClcvsvrEmA"}],"urlSource":"/ch04.intro","key":"VBo17GoIWH"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch04.schrodinger","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4.1 The Schrodinger Equation","key":"D8qmEbH4H7"}],"urlSource":"/ch04.schrodinger","key":"lcwTVm3eLF"}],"key":"PuCNKcrQ2f"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch04.operators","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4.2 Operators and Expectation Values","key":"UTVqXsuw95"}],"urlSource":"/ch04.operators","key":"keBRfDdCgg"}],"key":"hcQMq5Hg8l"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch04.hydrogen","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4.3 The Quantum Mechanical Solution of the Hydrogen Atom","key":"UJ4KSCy8EW"}],"urlSource":"/ch04.hydrogen","key":"a6d3dkgyVG"}],"key":"IPpa5uWgCR"}],"key":"z67jR9Cai9"}],"key":"oJWEGW4aBX"}],"key":"PVRhZ8NsWL"}],"key":"e7po8vaRyV"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 5: Metadata and Perturbations","key":"WXllmXng4u"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch05.intro","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chapter 5: Metadata and Perturbations","key":"U2WLO1gL7A"}],"urlSource":"/ch05.intro","key":"kIPOvBxhZo"},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch05.spin","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"5.1 Electron Spin","key":"IIdylFgkUd"}],"urlSource":"/ch05.spin","key":"jaJh19UMxa"}],"key":"Yf7b1jRQql"},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ch05.fine-structure","internal":true,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"5.2 Fine Structure and the Zeeman Effect","key":"yQ4VwckXmO"}],"urlSource":"/ch05.fine-structure","key":"Z5ZKNOc9Ej"}],"key":"KWQge5SlDe"}],"key":"ybN4ffBgFD"}],"key":"ECiyFqYBiC"}],"key":"yzIbQ8mXHw"}],"key":"AD3aLepvvR"}],"key":"PqhSEhzOlh"}],"data":{"part":"toc:project"},"key":"HR518bhc1o"}],"key":"zUI4IIAH1A"}],"key":"DNc5gmF1GD"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"2.5 The Franck-Hertz Experiment","url":"/ch02/franck-hertz","group":"Chapter 2: Digitization of Information"},"next":{"title":"3.1 Wave-Particle Duality","url":"/ch03/wave-particle","group":"Chapter 3: Logic Core Reconstruction"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3001"}